# write.bot -- the first real I/O program: Botlish writes to standard output
# through its own raw Linux x86-64 syscall path (ABI-BYTES.md):
#
#   String
#     --std::str::encode_utf8--------> List of bytes
#     --std::abi::bytes::from_list--------------> std::abi::bytes::Bytes      (owned, contiguous, opaque)
#     --std::linux::write------------> address + std::abi::Usize length
#     --std::abi::x86_64::from_*-----> Register64 words (rsi, rdx)
#     --std::linux::abi::x86_64::syscall-----> the x86-64 `syscall` instruction (rax = 1)
#     --> the kernel's write(2) --> file descriptor 1
#
# No libc `write`, no special runtime implementation of Linux write: the
# external effect is the generic syscall primitive with
#
#   rax = 1 (SYS_write)   rdi = fd   rsi = address of the payload
#   rdx = the payload's byte count
#
# `data` is an ordinary immutable value: it can be written again, aliased,
# stored, compared. The result of std::linux::write is the raw signed result of the
# kernel's write (the number of bytes written, or a negative -errno).
#
# Native backend only (cranelift, or a standalone executable from
# `tclsh9.0 main.tcl -emit-native-executable examples/linux/write.bot`); the
# Tcl backends refuse the syscall with the semantic error NATIVE-ONLY.
#
# stdout (native): Hello from Botlish!
# value  (native): 20, the 20 bytes written (main.tcl's "value:" line; an
#                  executable prints no value)

# A direct module (DIRECT-MODULES.md): the program calls std::linux::write, a
# callable of the direct module linux, at its top level -- a deliberately
# low-level program, which says so (examples/linux/context-hello.bot is the
# same output through std::linux::io's LinuxIO context, and is no direct module).
direct module

import std::abi
import std::abi::bytes
import std::linux
import std::str

data = std::abi::bytes::from_list(std::str::encode_utf8("Hello from Botlish!\n"))
std::abi::i32(1).write(data)
